# Config for VS Code's clangd language server ONLY. It does NOT affect the build —
# Xcode / xcodebuild compile this project with their own flags and report none of these.
#
# This is an Xcode + CocoaPods project. When clangd opens an Objective-C file on its own it has
# none of Xcode's build settings: no SDK sysroot, no framework/header search paths, and modules
# are off. So Foundation and CocoaLumberjack types — NSString, NSDate, NSMutableArray<NSArray *>,
# DDLogMessage, DDLogFileFormatterDefault, the ExamLogPhase enum, ... — all resolve to "unknown".
# The lightweight generics (NSMutableArray<...>) and the method signatures that use those types
# then fail to parse, and a single unresolved header cascades into a wall of bogus errors:
#   "Expected a type"              a type name clangd couldn't resolve (e.g. ExamLogPhase)
#   "Expected identifier or '('"   the <...> of an Objective-C generic on an unknown class
#   "Too many errors ... stopping" the cascade tripping clangd's error ceiling
#
# None of this reflects the real code — these files compile cleanly in Xcode and ship in the DMG.
# Properly teaching clangd this project would need a generated compile_commands.json for the whole
# CocoaPods workspace. Short of that, clangd's diagnostics here are pure noise, so they are turned
# off. Code navigation and completion still work. GENUINE problems still surface in Xcode and in
# `xcodebuild`, which is what actually compiles the project.
#
# NOTE: clangd caches this file. After changing it you MUST restart the server for it to take
# effect: Command Palette (Shift+Cmd+P) -> "clangd: Restart language server", or reload the window.
Diagnostics:
  Suppress: '*'
